/* data/detail-c/chem-11-02.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/chem-11-02.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-02"] = {"chem-11-02-b01":{"idea":"Different sub-atomic particles were found by different experiments. Thomson's cathode ray tube found the negative particle.","steps":["Thomson passed electricity through a gas at low pressure.","Rays moved from the cathode to the anode. They bent towards a positive plate.","So the rays were negatively charged particles. These are electrons."],"others":"Proton was found later by Goldstein's anode rays. Neutron was found by Chadwick. Positron is the positive twin of the electron and was found much later, in cosmic rays.","trap":"Remember: cathode rays = electrons, Thomson.","answer":"Electron"},"chem-11-02-b02":{"idea":"Mass number A is protons plus neutrons. Atomic number Z is the number of protons.","steps":["A = Z + N, so N = A − Z.","A = 35 and Z = 17.","N = 35 − 17 = 18."],"others":"17 is the number of protons, not neutrons. 35 is the mass number itself. 52 comes from adding A and Z instead of subtracting.","trap":"Neutrons = mass number minus atomic number.","answer":"18"},"chem-11-02-b03":{"idea":"Atomic number is the number of protons. It equals the mass number minus the neutrons.","steps":["Z = A − N.","A = 39 and N = 20.","Z = 39 − 20 = 19.","Check: the atom has 19 electrons and is neutral, so it has 19 protons too."],"trap":"A neutral atom has equal protons and electrons, so the 19 electrons confirm Z.","answer":"19"},"chem-11-02-b04":{"idea":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element. They have the same protons but a different number of neutrons.","steps":["The atomic number is the number of protons. It is the same for isotopes.","Mass number = protons + neutrons.","If protons are the same and the mass number differs, then the neutron numbers differ."],"others":"Electron number matches protons in a neutral atom, so it stays the same. Nuclear charge equals the proton number, which is fixed. A different proton number would make a different element.","trap":"Same Z, different A means different neutrons.","answer":"Different number of neutrons"},"chem-11-02-b05":{"idea":"The most electrons a shell holds is 2n², where n is the shell number.","steps":["Maximum electrons = 2n².","For n = 3, 2 × 3² = 2 × 9.","= 18 electrons."],"others":"8 is the limit for n = 2. 32 is the limit for n = 4. 9 is just n², which forgets the factor 2 for two spins.","trap":"Shell limits: 2, 8, 18, 32.","answer":"18"},"chem-11-02-b06":{"idea":"The number of orbitals in a shell is n². Each orbital holds two electrons.","steps":["Orbitals in shell n = n².","n = 2 gives 2² = 4 orbitals.","These are one 2s and three 2p orbitals."],"others":"2 counts only subshell types, not orbitals. 8 is the number of electrons that these four orbitals hold. 6 is the number of electrons in 2p alone.","trap":"Do not mix orbitals (n²) with electrons (2n²).","answer":"4"},"chem-11-02-b07":{"idea":"The azimuthal quantum number l tells the subshell: s = 0, p = 1, d = 2, f = 3.","steps":["The letter in 3p is p.","For a p subshell, l = 1.","So l = 1."],"others":"0 belongs to an s orbital. 2 belongs to a d orbital. 3 is the principal quantum number of this orbital, not l.","trap":"The digit is n. The letter gives l.","answer":"1"},"chem-11-02-b08":{"idea":"The spin quantum number ms describes the two possible spin directions of an electron.","steps":["An electron spins in one of two ways.","These two ways are given the values +1/2 and −1/2.","No other value is allowed."],"others":"0, 1 and −1 are not allowed, because the electron spin is always half. The pairs 0 and 1/2, and 0 and 1, include values that do not exist for ms.","trap":"ms is always ±1/2.","answer":"+1/2 and -1/2"},"chem-11-02-b09":{"idea":"Rutherford fired alpha particles at thin gold foil. How they scattered showed the structure of the atom.","steps":["Most alpha particles went straight through. So most of the atom is empty space.","A few bounced back at large angles. They hit something small, dense and positive.","This small centre is the nucleus."],"others":"A uniform sphere with electrons embedded is Thomson's model, which this experiment disproved. Neutrons were not known then. The indivisible atom is Dalton's idea, which was already disproved by Thomson.","trap":"Few deflections but a tiny nucleus: remember empty space.","answer":"Most of the atom's volume is empty space, with a small dense positively charged nucleus"},"chem-11-02-b10":{"idea":"Isobars are different elements with the same mass number. Isotopes share the atomic number, isotones share the neutron number.","steps":["Isobars have different atomic numbers, so they are different elements.","Their protons plus neutrons add up to the same total.","So they have the same mass number."],"others":"Same atomic number describes isotopes. Same number of neutrons describes isotones. Same number of electrons is not a type of relation, and for neutral atoms it would mean the same atomic number.","trap":"Isobar = same A. Isotope = same Z.","answer":"Mass number"},"chem-11-02-b11":{"idea":"The configuration shows how the electrons are spread over the orbitals. Fill in order of increasing energy: 1s, 2s, 2p.","steps":["Carbon has Z = 6, so 6 electrons.","1s holds 2 electrons. 2s holds 2 electrons.","The remaining 2 electrons go into 2p.","Configuration = 1s² 2s² 2p²."],"others":"1s² 2s² 2p⁴ has 8 electrons, which is oxygen. 1s² 2s⁴ is impossible, since an s subshell holds only 2. 1s⁴ 2s² is also impossible for the same reason.","trap":"Add up the superscripts. They must equal Z.","answer":"1s2 2s2 2p2"},"chem-11-02-b12":{"idea":"The Aufbau principle gives the filling order. It says that electrons occupy the lowest available energy first.","steps":["Aufbau is a German word for building up.","Electrons enter the orbital with the lowest energy that is free.","As electrons are added, they go to higher and higher energy orbitals."],"others":"Decreasing energy would put electrons in high orbitals first, which is unstable. Outermost shell inward is the opposite of the real order. Random filling ignores energy, and the Pauli principle is a separate rule.","trap":"Build from the bottom: lowest energy first.","answer":"In order of increasing orbital energy"},"chem-11-02-b13":{"idea":"Light obeys c = νλ. Frequency is speed divided by wavelength.","steps":["ν = c / λ.","λ = 600 nm = 600 × 10⁻⁹ m = 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ m.","ν = 3.0 × 10⁸ / 6.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz."],"trap":"Convert nm to m first: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.","answer":"5.0 (× 10¹⁴ Hz)"},"chem-11-02-b14":{"idea":"Planck's relation says a photon's energy is proportional to its frequency: E = hν.","steps":["E = hν, where h is Planck's constant.","h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and ν = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","E = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5.0 × 10¹⁴ = 33.13 × 10⁻²⁰ J.","E = 3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."],"trap":"The power of ten changes: −34 + 14 = −20, so write the answer as 3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹.","answer":"3.31 (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)"},"chem-11-02-b15":{"idea":"An orbital shape shows where an electron is most likely found. Each subshell type has its own shape.","steps":["An s orbital has l = 0.","Its probability cloud is the same in every direction.","So an s orbital is spherical."],"others":"Dumbbell shape belongs to p orbitals. Cloverleaf (double dumbbell) belongs to most d orbitals. A ring is not a shape of any of the main orbitals.","trap":"s = sphere, p = dumbbell, d = cloverleaf.","answer":"Spherical"},"chem-11-02-b16":{"idea":"The number of subshells in shell n equals n. Their l values run from 0 to n − 1.","steps":["For n = 4, l = 0, 1, 2, 3.","These are s, p, d and f.","So there are 4 subshells."],"others":"A g subshell (l = 4) would start only at n = 5. 3 leaves out f. 16 is the number of orbitals (n²), not subshells.","trap":"Subshells = n. Orbitals = n².","answer":"4 (s, p, d, f)"},"chem-11-02-b17":{"idea":"The allowed values of l run from 0 to n − 1. The value of ml runs from −l to +l.","steps":["Check each set in turn.","n = 2, l = 2 breaks the rule, since l can be at most n − 1 = 1.","The other three sets follow every rule.","So the set with n = 2, l = 2 is not allowed."],"others":"n = 2, l = 1, ml = −1 is a 2p orbital, allowed. n = 4, l = 0, ml = 0 is a 4s orbital, allowed. n = 3, l = 2, ml = −1 is a 3d orbital, allowed.","trap":"First test l < n.","answer":"n = 2, l = 2, ml = 0, ms = +1/2"},"chem-11-02-b18":{"idea":"Fill orbitals in the order 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p. Each s holds 2 and each p holds 6.","steps":["Chlorine has 17 electrons.","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ uses 10 electrons.","3s² uses 2 more, making 12.","The remaining 5 go to 3p.","Configuration: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵."],"others":"1s² 2s² 2p⁵ 3s² 3p⁶ leaves 2p unfilled while 3p is full. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹ 3p⁶ has 3s not full before 3p. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ has 18 electrons, which is argon.","trap":"Count the electrons: they must be 17.","answer":"1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p5"},"chem-11-02-b19":{"idea":"A positive ion has lost electrons. They are removed from the outermost shell.","steps":["Na has 11 electrons: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹.","Na⁺ has lost one electron, leaving 10.","The lost electron is the 3s electron.","Na⁺ = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶."],"others":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹ is the neutral Na atom. 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² has 12 electrons, which is Mg. 1s² 2s² 2p⁵ has 9 electrons, so it does not match Na⁺.","trap":"Cations lose electrons from the outermost shell.","answer":"1s2 2s2 2p6"},"chem-11-02-b20":{"idea":"Half-filled and fully filled subshells are extra stable. A few atoms move an electron to reach them.","steps":["Aufbau predicts Cr = [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s².","Moving one 4s electron to 3d gives 3d⁵ 4s¹.","Now five 3d orbitals each hold one electron: half-filled.","A half-filled subshell has symmetry and extra exchange energy, so it is more stable."],"others":"Chromium has 24 electrons, the usual number. Electrons of 4s can pair with 3d, so that is not the reason. 3d does not always fill first. For Z up to 20, 4s fills before 3d.","trap":"The exception only exists in Cr and Cu (in this class).","answer":"A half-filled 3d subshell (3d5) gives extra stability"},"chem-11-02-b21":{"idea":"A completely filled d subshell is especially stable. Copper moves one 4s electron to reach it.","steps":["Aufbau predicts Cu = [Ar] 3d⁹ 4s².","Moving one 4s electron to 3d gives 3d¹⁰ 4s¹.","3d¹⁰ is completely filled, which gives extra stability.","So Cu = [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹."],"others":"3d is not higher than 4s in every case, since in ions the order changes. 4s electrons being removed first is about ionisation, not about the ground state. Copper has 29 electrons, enough for 4s².","trap":"Full d¹⁰ and half-full d⁵ are the two stable cases.","answer":"A completely filled 3d subshell (3d10) gives extra stability"},"chem-11-02-b22":{"idea":"Hund's rule says that orbitals of equal energy are singly filled first, with parallel spins, before any pairing.","steps":["The 2p subshell has three orbitals.","Nitrogen has 3 electrons in 2p.","By Hund's rule, each orbital gets one electron.","So there are 3 unpaired electrons."],"others":"0 would mean all the electrons are paired, but 2p³ cannot pair without leaving an orbital empty. 1 and 2 would require pairing before each orbital is half-filled, which Hund's rule does not allow.","trap":"Draw three boxes with one arrow in each.","answer":"3"},"chem-11-02-b23":{"idea":"Frequency of light is found from c = νλ.","steps":["ν = c / λ.","λ = 450 nm = 4.50 × 10⁻⁷ m.","ν = 3.0 × 10⁸ / 4.50 × 10⁻⁷.","ν = 0.6667 × 10¹⁵ = 6.67 × 10¹⁴ Hz."],"trap":"Shorter wavelength means higher frequency. 450 nm gives a higher value than the 5.0 for 600 nm.","answer":"6.67 (× 10¹⁴ Hz)"},"chem-11-02-b24":{"idea":"A node is a place where the electron probability is zero. Total nodes = n − 1. Angular nodes = l. Radial nodes = n − l − 1.","steps":["For 3p: n = 3 and l = 1.","Angular nodes = l = 1.","Radial nodes = n − l − 1 = 3 − 1 − 1 = 1.","Total = 1 + 1 = 2 nodes."],"others":"0 would suit a 1s orbital. 1 would suit a 2p orbital, which has only one angular node. 3 would suit a 4s orbital.","trap":"Quick check: total nodes = n − 1 = 2.","answer":"2"},"chem-11-02-b25":{"idea":"Quantum numbers n and l fix a subshell. The subshell holds 2(2l + 1) electrons.","steps":["n = 3, l = 2 means the 3d subshell.","A d subshell has 2l + 1 = 5 orbitals.","Each orbital holds 2 electrons.","Maximum = 5 × 2 = 10 electrons."],"others":"2 is the capacity of one orbital. 6 is the capacity of a p subshell. 14 is the capacity of an f subshell.","trap":"d = 5 orbitals = 10 electrons.","answer":"10"},"chem-11-02-b26":{"idea":"Atoms with the same mass number but different atomic numbers are called isobars.","steps":["Both nuclei have mass number 40.","Their atomic numbers are 18 and 20, which differ.","Same A and different Z means isobars. (Ar-40 and Ca-40)"],"others":"Isotopes need the same atomic number, which is not true here. Isotones need the same neutron number. Here they are 22 and 20, which differ. Isomers are for molecules with the same formula but different structure.","trap":"Isobars: A equal.","answer":"Isobars"},"chem-11-02-b27":{"idea":"Compare protons, neutrons and mass number of both nuclei to name the relation.","steps":["First nucleus: 20 protons, 20 neutrons, A = 40.","Second nucleus: 19 protons, 21 neutrons, A = 40.","The mass numbers are equal, but the proton numbers differ.","This is the definition of isobars."],"others":"They are not isotopes, because the proton numbers 20 and 19 differ. They are not isotones, because the neutron numbers 20 and 21 differ. They are not identical elements, since different Z means different elements.","trap":"Total protons + neutrons is 40 for both.","answer":"Isobars (same mass number)"},"chem-11-02-b28":{"idea":"The Pauli exclusion principle says no two electrons in an atom have the same four quantum numbers.","steps":["Electrons in one orbital share n, l and ml.","So they must differ in ms.","ms has only two values.","So an orbital holds a maximum of 2 electrons."],"others":"1 would waste the second spin state. 4 and 6 are more than the two spin values allow in a single orbital. 6 is the capacity of a p subshell, not one orbital.","trap":"One orbital = two electrons with opposite spins.","answer":"2"},"chem-11-02-b29":{"idea":"De Broglie said that matter has wave nature too. Every moving particle has a wavelength.","steps":["The wavelength is given by λ = h / (mv).","Here h is Planck's constant, m the mass and v the speed.","This is true for electrons and other particles, though it is only noticeable for tiny masses."],"others":"Fixed circular paths are Bohr's model. Energy as continuous flow is the old classical idea that Planck replaced. Only photons having waves is wrong, because de Broglie extended wave properties to matter.","trap":"λ = h / mv, so heavy objects have tiny wavelengths.","answer":"Every moving particle has an associated wavelength, lambda = h/(mv)"},"chem-11-02-b30":{"idea":"The de Broglie wavelength of a moving particle is λ = h / (mv).","steps":["λ = h / (mv).","m = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg and v = 2.0 × 10⁶ m/s.","mv = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁶ = 1.822 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m/s.","λ = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ / 1.822 × 10⁻²⁴ = 3.64 × 10⁻¹⁰ m."],"trap":"Wavelength of the same size as an atom: this is why electrons give diffraction patterns.","answer":"3.64 (× 10⁻¹⁰ m)"},"chem-11-02-b31":{"idea":"The uncertainty principle says there is a limit to how well two linked quantities can be known together.","steps":["It was stated by Heisenberg.","It says Δx × Δp ≥ h / 4π, where x is position and p is momentum.","If position is known very sharply, momentum becomes very uncertain, and the other way round."],"others":"Mass and charge are fixed properties and can both be known. Protons and neutrons in a nucleus can be counted. Energy and identity of an element are not a pair of linked variables.","trap":"The pair is position and momentum.","answer":"Both the position and momentum of a particle"},"chem-11-02-b32":{"idea":"In the photoelectric effect, a photon must have enough energy to free an electron. Energy depends on frequency, not on brightness.","steps":["Each photon has energy E = hν.","If ν is below the threshold frequency, each photon has too little energy.","One electron absorbs one photon only, so photon energies do not add up.","So no electrons are ejected, however bright the light."],"others":"Negative kinetic energy cannot occur for a free electron. Slow ejection also needs enough photon energy. Very high intensity only means more photons, each still too weak.","trap":"Frequency decides the energy. Intensity decides the number of photons.","answer":"No electrons are ejected, regardless of light intensity"},"chem-11-02-b33":{"idea":"Each orbital type has its own shape: s, p and d. The p orbital has two lobes.","steps":["A p orbital has l = 1.","It has two lobes on opposite sides of the nucleus.","The shape is a dumbbell.","There is a node (zero probability) at the nucleus."],"others":"Four lobes in one plane is the d orbital shape. A sphere is the s orbital. A flat ring is not an orbital shape asked for in this syllabus (it is closer to the dz² collar).","trap":"p = dumbbell, with a node at the centre.","answer":"Dumbbell: two lobes on opposite sides of the nucleus"},"chem-11-02-b34":{"idea":"The principal quantum number n gives the shell number. The l value gives the letter: 0 = s, 1 = p, 2 = d, 3 = f.","steps":["n = 4 gives the number 4.","l = 2 gives the letter d.","The orbital is 4d."],"others":"3d has the right l but the wrong n. 4p has the right n but l = 1. 4f has the right n but l = 3.","trap":"Combine the n digit with the l letter.","answer":"4d"},"chem-11-02-b35":{"idea":"Shell capacity is 2n², where n is the shell number.","steps":["Maximum electrons = 2n².","For n = 2, 2 × 2² = 2 × 4.","= 8 electrons."],"trap":"The 2s holds 2 and the 2p holds 6. Together they hold 8.","answer":"8"},"chem-11-02-b36":{"idea":"Hund's rule says electrons spread out in equal-energy orbitals before they pair up.","steps":["Degenerate orbitals have the same energy, like the three 2p orbitals.","Each gets one electron first, all with the same spin.","Pairing starts only after every orbital has one electron."],"others":"Filling by n only is a part of the Aufbau idea, not Hund's rule. Pauli's principle is the one about four quantum numbers. Pairing up first in the lowest orbital is the opposite of what Hund's rule says.","trap":"Singly first, with parallel spins: like passengers taking empty seats first.","answer":"Degenerate orbitals are singly occupied with parallel spins before any pairing occurs"},"chem-11-02-b37":{"idea":"Fill the orbitals in order of energy: 1s, 2s, 2p. The s subshell holds 2 and the p subshell holds 6.","steps":["Oxygen has Z = 8, so 8 electrons.","1s² takes 2 and 2s² takes 2.","The remaining 4 go to 2p.","Configuration = 1s² 2s² 2p⁴."],"others":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ has 10 electrons, which is neon. 1s² 2s² 2p³ 3s¹ has 3s occupied before 2p is full. 1s² 2s⁴ 2p² is impossible, since 2s holds only 2.","trap":"Add the superscripts: 2 + 2 + 4 = 8.","answer":"1s2 2s2 2p4"},"chem-11-02-b38":{"idea":"Hund's rule: the 3 electrons in a p subshell go into 3 different orbitals, each unpaired.","steps":["Phosphorus has 3s² 3p³ in the outer shell.","3s² is a full orbital with a pair.","3p has three orbitals and three electrons.","Each p orbital gets one electron, so 3 unpaired."],"others":"0 is for a full or empty subshell. 1 would suit an atom like sodium. 5 is more unpaired electrons than 3p³ can have, since only three p orbitals exist in the outer shell.","trap":"Count only the p electrons, since s is paired.","answer":"3"},"chem-11-02-b39":{"idea":"The digit in the orbital name is n. The letter gives l: s = 0, p = 1, d = 2, f = 3.","steps":["In 5f, the digit is 5, so n = 5.","The letter f means l = 3.","So (n, l) = (5, 3)."],"others":"n = 3, l = 5 swaps the two numbers, and l must be less than n. n = 5, l = 4 would be a 5g orbital. n = 4, l = 3 would be a 4f orbital.","trap":"Digit = n. Letter = l.","answer":"n = 5, l = 3"},"chem-11-02-b40":{"idea":"In the Bohr model, the energy for each n is Eₙ = −13.6 / n² eV. Excitation needs the difference between two levels.","steps":["E₁ = −13.6 / 1² = −13.6 eV.","E₂ = −13.6 / 2² = −3.4 eV.","ΔE = E₂ − E₁ = −3.4 − (−13.6).","ΔE = 10.2 eV."],"trap":"The answer must be positive because energy is absorbed.","answer":"10.20 eV"},"chem-11-02-b41":{"idea":"Bohr said that the angular momentum of the electron is quantised. It can only be a whole number times h/2π.","steps":["Angular momentum is mvr.","Bohr's condition: mvr = n × h / (2π).","Here n is 1, 2, 3 ... the orbit number."],"others":"mvr = h / (2πn) puts n in the wrong place. mvr = nh leaves out the factor 2π. mv = nh / (2πr) is the same relation rearranged wrongly, since r moves to the wrong side.","trap":"n multiplies h/2π.","answer":"mvr = nh/(2.pi)"},"chem-11-02-b42":{"idea":"A full quantum number set needs four values. Each one has an allowed range.","steps":["For l = 1, ml can be −1, 0 or +1. For ml = 0 it is fixed.","ms can only be +1/2 or −1/2.","So only the set with ml = 0 and ms = +1/2 fits."],"others":"ml = 2 is outside the range −1 to +1. ms = +1 is not allowed, since ms is only ±1/2. l = 0 does not match the given orbital, which has l = 1.","trap":"Check n, l, ml, ms one by one.","answer":"n=3, l=1, ml=0, ms=+1/2"},"chem-11-02-b43":{"idea":"In Bohr's model, the orbit radius grows with n². For hydrogen Z = 1.","steps":["rₙ = 0.529 × n² / Z Å.","n = 2 and Z = 1.","r₂ = 0.529 × 4 / 1 = 2.116 Å.","To 2 decimal places this is 2.12 Å."],"trap":"The second orbit is four times the first orbit (0.529 Å).","answer":"2.12 Å"},"chem-11-02-b44":{"idea":"The photoelectric equation is KE = hν − W. W is the work function. Two readings let us solve for W.","steps":["KE₁ = hν₁ − W and KE₂ = hν₂ − W.","Subtract: 3.3 − 1.5 = h × (1.2 × 10¹⁵ − 8.0 × 10¹⁴).","1.8 eV = h × 4.0 × 10¹⁴, so h = 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV s.","W = hν₁ − KE₁ = 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 8.0 × 10¹⁴ − 1.5.","W = 3.6 − 1.5 = 2.1 eV."],"trap":"Using the second reading must give the same W: 5.4 − 3.3 = 2.1.","answer":"2.1 eV"},"chem-11-02-b45":{"idea":"Cr and Cu are the two common exceptions. They gain stability from a half-filled or fully filled 3d subshell.","steps":["Cr moves one 4s electron to get 3d⁵ 4s¹ (half-filled).","Cu moves one 4s electron to get 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ (full).","So the right pair is Cr 3d⁵ 4s¹ and Cu 3d¹⁰ 4s¹."],"others":"3d⁵ 4s¹ with 3d⁹ 4s² gives only Cr as an exception. 3d⁴ 4s² with 3d⁹ 4s² are the Aufbau predictions, so there is no exception. 3d⁶ 4s⁰ for Cr is wrong, since the 4s electron is not removed entirely.","trap":"Both exceptions have 4s¹.","answer":"Cr: [Ar]3d5 4s1; Cu: [Ar]3d10 4s1"},"chem-11-02-b46":{"idea":"Shell capacity is 2n². The n = 4 shell holds the 4s, 4p, 4d and 4f subshells.","steps":["Maximum electrons = 2n².","For n = 4, 2 × 16 = 32.","Check: 2 + 6 + 10 + 14 = 32."],"trap":"Do not confuse 32 electrons with 16 orbitals.","answer":"32"},"chem-11-02-b47":{"idea":"An electron accelerated through a voltage V gains energy eV. Its de Broglie wavelength then follows from λ = h / √(2meV).","steps":["Compute 2meV = 2 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100.","2meV = 2.915 × 10⁻⁴⁷.","√(2meV) = 5.40 × 10⁻²⁴.","λ = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ / 5.40 × 10⁻²⁴ = 1.227 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.","1 Å = 10⁻¹⁰ m, so λ = 1.227 Å."],"trap":"A shortcut: λ ≈ 12.27 / √V Å, which gives 1.227 Å for V = 100.","answer":"1.227 Å"},"chem-11-02-b48":{"idea":"The Rydberg formula gives the wavelength of a hydrogen line: 1/λ = R (1/n₁² − 1/n₂²). Lyman lines end at n₁ = 1.","steps":["R = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹, n₁ = 1 and n₂ = 3.","1/λ = 1.097 × 10⁷ × (1 − 1/9) = 1.097 × 10⁷ × 8/9.","1/λ = 9.75 × 10⁶ m⁻¹.","λ = 1.026 × 10⁻⁷ m = 102.6 nm."],"trap":"This is in the ultraviolet, as every Lyman line is.","answer":"102.6 nm"},"chem-11-02-b49":{"idea":"The atomic number equals the total number of electrons in a neutral atom. Add up the whole configuration.","steps":["[Ar] stands for the 18 electrons of argon.","3d¹⁰ adds 10, making 28.","4s² adds 2, making 30.","4p³ adds 3, making 33.","Atomic number = 33 (arsenic)."],"others":"35 would be bromine with 4p⁵. 31 would be gallium with 4p¹. 18 is only the argon core.","trap":"Do not forget the 18 electrons inside [Ar].","answer":"33"},"chem-11-02-b50":{"idea":"A transition metal loses its 4s electrons first. Then it loses d electrons if more are needed.","steps":["Cu = [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹.","Cu²⁺ loses two electrons.","The first one comes from 4s. The second comes from 3d.","Cu²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁹."],"others":"3d¹⁰ 4s⁻¹ is meaningless, as an orbital cannot hold a negative number of electrons. 3d⁸ 4s² has the right total only if 4s were refilled, and it removes three electrons. 4s¹ 3d⁸ keeps the 4s electron and loses two from d, which is not the usual order.","trap":"The 4s electrons leave before the 3d electrons.","answer":"[Ar] 3d9"}};
